<worksheet xml:id="port-problems-408">
  <title>Portfolio Project Problems - Math 408</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Our goal in this problem is to prove that it is impossible to order the set of complex numbers <m>\mathbb{C}</m> in such a way that <m>\mathbb{C}</m> is an ordered field.  In order to do this, you will first need to prove some results for ordered fields.  So for the following problems, assume that <m>A</m> is an ordered field with at least two elements, that <m>0 \in A</m> is the additive identity of <m>A</m>, and that <m>1 \in A</m> is the multiplicative identity in <m>A</m>.  Using only the axioms for ordered fields, prove the following.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>0 \ne 1</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>0 &lt; 1</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>-1 &lt; 0</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>a,b \in A</m>, then <m>(-a)(-b)=ab</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        After you prove the above, use them to prove that no matter how you order the elements of <m>\mathbb{C}</m>, it is impossible to make <m>\mathbb{C}</m> an ordered field.  Use your intuition to give you insight into these problems, but do not use your intuition as justifications in your proof.  You are to use only the ordered field axioms.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>I</m> be an interval that is bounded above and bounded below. Prove that <m>I=[a,b]</m>, <m>[a,b)</m>, <m>(a,b]</m>, or <m>(a,b)</m> for some real numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.  For this problem only, you may use the fact that any set of real numbers that is bounded below has an infimum (which follows from the completeness axiom).
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>S</m> is an infinite set (maybe countably infinite, maybe not), and let <m>T = \{ x_1,x_2, \ldots , x_n \}</m> (i.e. <m>T</m> is a finite set) that is disjoint from <m>S</m>.  Prove that <m>S</m> and <m>S \cup T</m> have the same cardinality.  In other words, prove there is a function <m>f: S \rightarrow (S \cup T)</m> that is one-to-one and onto. Hint:  Warm up by solving the problem for the case that <m>S</m> is countably infinite, and then do the case where <m>S</m> is uncountable.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\{ x_n \}</m> be a bounded sequence that does not converge.  Prove that <m>\{ x_n \}</m> has at least two subsequences that converge to different limits.   Note that both sequences need to converge.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For this problem, we are assuming that the real numbers are as we constructed in class.  In other words, <m>\mathbb{R}</m> is the set of equivalence classes of Cauchy sequences of rational numbers that are derived from the following relation:  <m>\{ x_n \} \sim \{ y_n \}</m> if and only if <m>\{ x_n - y_n \}</m> converges to 0.  We are also using our definitions of addition, multiplication, and <m>&lt;</m> on <m>\mathbb{R}</m> that we determined in class.  Prove the following.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Multiplication is well defined.  In other words, if <m>X,Y \in \mathbb{R}</m>, prove that no matter how you choose <m>\{ x_n \} \in X</m> and <m>\{ y_n \} \in Y</m> to compute <m>X \cdot Y</m>, you always get the same product.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbb{R}</m> is closed under addition.  In other words, for all <m>X,Y \in \mathbb{R}</m>, if <m>X,Y \in \mathbb{R}</m>, prove that <m>X+Y \in \mathbb{R}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\mathbb{R}</m> is closed under multiplication.  In other words, for all <m>X,Y \in \mathbb{R}</m>, if <m>X,Y \in \mathbb{R}</m>, prove that <m>X \cdot Y \in \mathbb{R}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Multiplicative Inverse:  Let <m>Z</m> be the additive identity and <m>I</m> be the multiplicative identity of <m>\mathbb{R}</m>. Prove that for each <m>X \in \mathbb{R}</m> with <m>X \ne Z</m>, there exists some <m>Y \in \mathbb{R}</m> such that <m>X \cdot Y=I</m>.  You do <alert>NOT</alert> need to do a full proof that your <m>Y</m> is a real number.  Just do enough computations and explanation to show why it is a real number.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The order relation <m>&lt;</m> is well defined.  In other words, if <m>X,Y \in \mathbb{R}</m>, we will get the same determination of <m>X &lt; Y</m>, <m>Y &lt; X</m>, or <m>X=Y</m> regardless of which sequences we choose from <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m>. Hint: You are likely to prove this using proof by contradiction.  When you set up your contradiction, I suggest the following.
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Express your contradiction assumption as inequalities.  You will likely have four inequalities to play with.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Set up your inequalities so that they do not have any absolute value signs in them.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Play with your inequalities until you find your contradiction.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Trichotomy: For all <m>X,Y \in \mathbb{R}</m>, either <m>X &lt; Y</m>, <m>X=Y</m>, or <m>Y &lt; X</m>. Hint: You want to eliminate the possibility that none of <m>X &lt; Y</m>, <m>Y &lt; X</m>, and <m>X=Y</m> are true.
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Use #2 on Homework #5 to get an <q><m>N</m></q> large enough to get a useful inequality between the terms of your representative sequences from <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Choose your <q><m>N</m></q> to be large enough so that the terms of each of your sequences are close together.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    If <m>N</m> is past all the above <q><m>N</m>'s</q>, you know that either <m>x_{N+1} \le y_{N+1}</m> or <m>y_{N+1} \le x_{N+1}</m>. Show that for all <m>n &gt; N</m>, <m>x_n</m> and <m>y_n</m> have to keep the same order relationship as <m>x_{N+1}</m> and <m>y_{N+1}</m>.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        Please remember that your Cauchy sequences can only contain rational numbers.  You may not assume that all Cauchy sequences converge, since that does not make sense if the sequence <q>converges</q> to an irrational number.  You may assume that a Cauchy sequence is bounded.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>p \in \mathbb{R}</m> be a constant, and let <m>f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> be a continuous function such that <m>f(x+p) = f(x)</m> for all <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m> (such a function is called periodic).  Prove that <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>. Hint:  First consider <m>f</m> on <m>[0,p]</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>